PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The core legal issue is Ontario Sale of Goods Act s. 19 r. 2: for specific goods the seller must put into deliverable state, property/risk passes only when (1) seller completes the work and (2) buyer receives notice that it has been done. On June 2 the MacBook was not deliverable (screen still needed replacement). Jake did finish the repair, but his June 5 text stated only that work was “almost finished” and would be ready the next day; this is not notice of completion. Flood occurred overnight June 5-6, so risk remained with Jake and he must refund. 

Disagreement: Colleagues 1/5/7/8/9/12/13/14 (B) treat the June 5 text as sufficient notice once repairs were factually done; colleagues 2/3/4/6/10/11 (C) correctly insist the statute requires confirmatory notice of completion, not prospective “almost done” language. A, D, E are inconsistent with the statute (no insurance overlay, no requirement of physical delivery, and laptop was not deliverable on June 2). 

Current lean: C. No unresolved factual disputes; only interpretive split on whether the text satisfied the notice rule.}

