PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core legal issue remains Ontario Sale of Goods Act s. 19 r. 2: specific goods needing seller work to reach deliverable state; risk passes only on (1) completion of work + (2) buyer receiving notice that work “has been done.” June 2 MacBook not deliverable (screen repair pending). June 5 text used prospective language (“almost finished … will be ready tomorrow”), not confirmatory notice of completion; flood occurred before risk shifted, so Jake must refund. 

Round 1 shifts: Colleague 12 moved from B to C; B-group (1/5/7/9/13/14) now stresses contextual/commercial reading, buyer’s acceptance of timeline, and that statute does not demand hyper-literal post-completion phrasing. C-group (2/3/4/6/8/10/11 +12) uniformly insists statutory wording requires explicit confirmatory notice, not “almost done + pickup date.” No new facts, cases, or statutory language introduced; disagreement is purely interpretive. A/D/E still rejected by all as inconsistent with Rule 2. 

My position unchanged: C. Statutory text and timing control; “almost finished” message fails notice requirement. Persistent open question: whether any Ontario case law relaxes the “has been done” phrasing in practice (none cited so far).}

